    answer: `The Calabash River is tidal, and the humidity it holds over these golf-community lots keeps the infill damp enough, overnight, that odor bacteria rarely get a real dry stretch to die back on their own. TIMELESS Turf Restoration flushes the pet zone, applies an enzyme treatment that breaks down the residue and an antimicrobial to slow the bacteria, included in a Premium Restoration from $299.`,
    blocks: [
      { h2: 'One dog, one yard, for years', html: `<p>Calabash's median age is 56.4 and a third of residents are 65 or older, which is a different pattern than a rental beach town: mostly one dog, the same owner, the same corner of turf, for years at a time. That long a routine means residue has time to work deep into the infill rather than washing out with a season's rain. VCA Seaside Animal Hospital handles boarding and daycare locally, which keeps some daytime load off the yard, but the routine bathroom use still concentrates in one spot. A single-dog household also means that spot rarely moves once we've found it, which is why a follow-up visit here usually goes faster than the first.</p>` },
      { h2: 'A golf town, not a rental one', html: `<p>Calabash draws plenty of day-trip visitors for its seafood, but the housing around Brunswick Plantation, Crow Creek and Meadowlands isn't rental stock the way it is a few miles up the coast. That matters for a pet-odor visit: we're treating the same dog's routine on every return trip, not guessing at whichever animal a week's renters brought along, so the enzyme and antimicrobial treatment can be tuned to what's actually there rather than reset each visit.</p>` },
      { h2: 'River humidity keeps the bacteria working', html: `<p>The Calabash River is tidal, and the humidity it holds over Brunswick Plantation, Crow Creek and Meadowlands lots keeps the pet zone damp longer overnight than an inland yard would be. Moisture is what reactivates the bacteria that turn urine into ammonia, so a Calabash yard rarely gets the fully dry stretch that lets a mild smell fade on its own. <a href="/services/pet-odor-removal/">Pet odor removal</a> here pairs the enzyme treatment with an antimicrobial specifically because of that.</p>` },
      { h2: 'Not the water crust, the infill', html: `<p>Brunswick County Public Utilities publishes a hardness of 28.07 for Calabash, moderately soft, so a lingering smell here isn't mineral buildup from the tap. It's residue and bacteria in the infill, which is what enzyme and antimicrobial treatment address directly rather than a deodorizer spray.</p>` },
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: `Does Calabash's water hardness affect pet odor treatment?`, a: `No. Brunswick County Public Utilities publishes a hardness of 28.07, moderately soft, so a lingering smell here is residue and bacteria in the infill, not something coming out of the tap.` },
      { q: `Does the Calabash River's humidity make pet odor worse?`, a: `Yes. The tidal river keeps ground-level humidity higher overnight on nearby lots, and moisture is what reactivates the bacteria behind the smell, so the pet zone rarely gets a fully dry break.` },
    ],
